#: {{coordinate terms|en|blusterous|blustery|cloudy|gusty|rainy|thundery|windy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Alexander Barclay|chapter=[The Argument]|mainauthor=w:Sebastian Brant|translator=Alexander Barclay|editor=[[w:Thomas Hill Jamieson|T[homas] H[ill] Jamieson]]|title=w:The Ship of Fools|location=Edinburgh|publisher=William Paterson; London: [[w:Sotheran's|Henry Sotheran & Co.]]|year='''1509''' December 24 (Gregorian calendar)|year_published=1874|volume=I|page=18|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/shipoffools01bran/page/18/mode/1up|oclc=1314328248|passage=I wyl aduertise you that this Boke is named the Shyp of foles of the worlde: For this worlde is nought els but a '''tempestous''' se in the whiche we dayly wander and are caste in dyuers tribulacions paynes and aduersitees: some by ignoraunce and some by wilfulnes: wherfore such doers ar worthy to be called foles.}}
#* {{RQ:Camden Holland Britain|chapter=Huntingdonshire|page=501|passage=[A] turbulent and '''tempeſtuous''' ſtorme aroſe, that encloſed them on everie ſide, ſo that laying aſide all hope, they vvere in utter deſpaire of their life, ſecuritie, or any helpe at all.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=I|scene=i|page=1|column=1|passage=A '''tempeſtuous''' noiſe of Thunder and Lightning heard: Enter a Ship-maſter, and a Boteſvvaine.|footer=A stage direction; the scene takes place aboard a ship at sea.}}
#* {{RQ:Bentley Confutation of Atheism|sermon=World 3|pages=25–26|pageref=25|passage=[T]he Months of ''March'' and ''September'', the tvvo Æquinoxes of Our year, are the moſt vvindy and '''tempeſtuous''', the moſt unſettled and unequable of Seaſons in moſt Countries of the VVorld.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Virgil|chapter=Book the Second|translator=w:Joseph Trapp|title=[[w:Aeneid|The Æneis of Virgil, Translated into Blank Verse]]|location=London|publisher=&#91;{{glossary|s.n.}}&#93;|year=1718|volume=I|lines=130–132|page=54|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pV_nAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA54|oclc=1328764187|passage=But oft the Sea / '''Tempeſtuous''' kept them back, and Southern VVinds / Deter'd them.}}
#* {{RQ:Thomson Winter|year=1730|lines=177–182|page=200|passage={{smallcaps|Huge Uproar}} lords it vvide. The clouds commixt / VVith ſtars ſvvift-gliding ſvveep along the sky. / All nature reels. Till nature's {{smallcaps|King}}, vvho oft / Amid '''tempeſtuous''' darkneſs dvvells alone, / And on the vvings of the careering vvind / VValks dreadfully ſerene, commands a calm; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:William Cobbett|title=Naples|newspaper=[[w:Political Register|Cobbett’s Weekly Political Register]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} C[harles Robert] Clement [for] John M. Cobbett,{{nb...|1 Clement’s Inn.}}|date=24 March 1821|year_published=12 March 1825|volume=38|issue=12|column=828|columnurl=|oclc=233639148|passage=And now, he has got us, or, rather, has got himself and the Honourable House, riding on a stormy and '''tempestous''' wave and seated upon a short and narrow Plank; a situation that I really could wish to see nobody in; no, not even (when I recollect what '''tempestous''' waves are) the punning Orator himself; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George Muir|chapter=The Coursing Match. Canto I.|title=The Sports of the Field,{{nb...|Containing the Biggar Annual Prize Coursing Match, and, the Kellburn Fox Chase through Lanark Streets.}}|location=Lanark, Lanarkshire|publisher={{...|Printed by}} George Roberton|year=1831|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iROgyw0MkgQC&pg=PA10|oclc=54188668|passage=The day was '''tempestous''' with rain and with wind, / Even chargers were seen to draw back; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Frederic Augustus Lucas|Frederic A[ugustus] Lucas]]|title=Notes of a Bird Catcher|editor=[[w:Joel Asaph Allen|J[oel] A[saph] Allen]]|journal=[[w:Ornithology (journal)|The Auk: A Quarterly Journal of Ornithology]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=L. S. Foster for the [[w:American Ornithological Society|American Ornithologists’ Union]]|month=January|year=1887|volume=IV (New Series; volume XII overall)|issue=I|pages=1–2|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/auk41887amer/page/1/mode/1up|issn=0004-8038|oclc=802434110|passage=Like all other sea birds, Albatrosses can be most easily enticed into biting during '''tempestous''' weather when, having been prevented for days from procuring their regular amount of food, the pangs of hunger overcome their natural distrust.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George H. Hart, attorney for the plaintiff-respondent; Daniel Sullivan, plaintiff’s witness|title=[[w:New York Supreme Court, Appellate Division|Appellate Division of the Supreme Court]], in the First Judicial Department. Catherine Kay, Plaintiff-Respondent, vs. Metropolitan Street Railway Company, Defendant-Appellant. Case on Appeal|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Douglas Taylor & Co.,{{nb...|law printers, 8 Warren Street.}}|year=1898|page=223|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OnuixszMPN4C&pg=PA223|passage=Q. The night of this accident was an extremely '''tempestuous''' night, was it not? A. Well, it was a kind of a snow storm that night. {{...}} I would regard such a night as a '''tempestuous''' night.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Return of Sherlock Holmes|story=The Adventure of the Golden Pince-Nez|page=262|passage=It was a wild, '''tempestuous''' night towards the close of November. {{...}} Outside the wind howled down Baker Street, while the rain beat fiercely against the windows. It was strange there in the very depths of the town, with ten miles of man's handiwork on every side of us, to feel the iron grip of Nature, and to be conscious that to the huge elemental forces all London was no more than the molehills that dot the fields.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Donovan Hohn|chapter=Going Overboard|title=[[w:Moby-Duck|Moby-Duck: The True Story of 28,800 Bath Toys Lost at Sea]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:The Quarto Group|Union Books]]|year=2012|pages=9–10|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mobyducktruestor0000hohn/page/9/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-90-852602-1|passage=We know that the ship departed Hong Kong on January 6, that it arrived in the Port of Tacoma on January 16, a day behind schedule, and that the likely cause for this delay was rough weather. {{...}} [O]n January 10, the ''Ever Laurel'' did not fax a weather report to the National Weather Service in Washington, D.C., but the following morning a ship in its vicinity did, describing hurricane-force winds and waves thirty-six feet high. If the ''Ever Laurel'' had encountered similarly '''tempestuous''' conditions, we can imagine, if only vaguely, what might have transpired: despite its grandeur, rocked by waves as tall as brownstones, the colossal vessel—a floating warehouse weighing 28,904 deadweight tons and powered by a diesel engine the size of a barn—would have rolled and pitched and yawed about like a toy in a Jacuzzi.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[characterize|Characterized]] by [[disorderly]], [[frenetic]], or [[violent#Adjective|violent]] [[activity]]; stormy, [[tumultuous]], [[turbulent]]; also, of a [[person#Noun|person]], their [[behavior|behaviour]] or [[nature#Noun|nature]], etc.: characterized by [[bout#Noun|bouts]] of [[bad#Adjective|bad]] [[temper#Noun|temper]] or [[sudden#Adjective|sudden]] [[change#Noun|changes]] of [[mood#Noun|mood]]; [[impetuous]], stormy, [[temperamental]].
#: {{antonyms|en|untempestuous}}
#: {{ux|en|After their '''tempestuous''' argument, they did not speak to each other for weeks.}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Psalms|psalm=XXXI|subtitle=In te, Domine, speravi|page=50|passage=Yet I confesse in that '''tempestious''' hast {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', haste}}, / I said, that I from out thy sight was cast: / But thou didst heare, when I to thee did moane, / Then love the Lord all ye that feele his grace: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Camden Holland Britain|chapter=Romans in Britaine|page=80|passage=[H]e vvould not ſuffer any farther inquiſition and examination to be had of the conſpirators, for feare leſt by ſtriking a terror among ſo many, the '''tempeſtuous''' troubles of the provinces vvhich vvere vvell appeaſed might revive again.}}
#* {{RQ:Herrick Hesperides|poem=Delight in Disorder|page=29|passage=A Svveet diſorder in the dreſſe / Kindles in cloathes a vvantonneſſe: / {{...}} / A vvinning vvave (deſerving Note) / In the '''tempeſtuous''' petticote: / {{...}} Doe more bevvitch me, then vvhen Art / Is too preciſe in every part.}}
#* {{RQ:Davenant Siege of Rhodes|page=4|passage=VVhat various Noiſes do mines{{sic}} ears invade? / And have a Conſort made? / The ſhriller Trumpet, and '''tempeſtuous''' Drum: / The deaf'ning clamor from the Canons vvombe; / VVhich through the Air like ſuddain Thunder breaks, / Seems calm to Souldiers ſhouts and VVomens ſhrieks.}}
#* {{RQ:Davenant Siege of Rhodes|part=2|page=23|passage=I at night / In vain ſeek Sleep vvith a '''tempeſtuous''' VVife. / VVink at my ſhame, that I, vvhoſe Banners brave / The vvorld, ſhould thus to Beauty be a Slave.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Cicero|Marcus Tullius Cicero]]|chapter=Letter XXVI. To [[w:Quintus Ligarius|Ligarius]].|translator=[[w:William Melmoth the younger|William Melmoth]]|title=[[w:Epistulae ad Familiares|The Letters of Marcus Tullius Cicero to Several of His Friends:{{nb...|With Remarks by William Melmoth, Esq;}}]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Robert Dodsley|R[obert] Dodsley]]{{nb...|in Pall-mall.}}|year=1753|volume=II|section=book IX|pages=366–367|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_2RT3XHXH-YC&pg=PA366|oclc=13601647|passage=Preſerve then, my friend, a firm and vigorous frame of mind: and if you bore the dark and '''tempeſtuous''' ſeaſon of your affairs vvith fortitude, let their preſent more ſerene and favorable aſpect fill your heart vvith chearfulneſs.}}
#* {{RQ:Burney Cecilia|volume=IV|chapter=A Perturbation|page=205|passage=Cecilia vvas ſtill in this '''tempeſtuous''' ſtate, vvhen a meſſage vvas brought her that a gentleman vvas belovv ſtairs, vvho begged to have the honour of ſeeing her.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray English Humourists|page=44|passage=[T]he brightest part of [[w:Jonathan Swift|{{quote-gloss|Jonathan}} Swift]]'s story, the pure star in that dark and '''tempestuous''' life of Swift's, is his love for Hester Johnson {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', {{w|Esther Johnson}}}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Swinburne Atalanta|page=47|passage=Sing, mix the wind with clamour, smite and shake / Sonorous timbrels and tumultuous hair, / And fill the dance up with '''tempestuous''' feet, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Frank O'Hara|Frank O’Hara]]|chapter=Poem|editor=w:Donald Allen|title=The Collected Poems of Frank O’Hara|location=Berkeley&#59; Los Angeles, Calif.&#59; London|publisher=w:University of California Press|year=a. '''1967''' (date written)<!--year after author's death-->|year_published=1995|page=211|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1evY4wjjy0C&pg=PA211|isbn=978-0-520-20166-8|passage='''Tempestuous''' breaths! we watch a girl / walking in her garden—no flowers, a wintry shrub / and the cold clouds passing over. {{...}} and through the cloth against her flesh / she feels the flashes of our heat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Shelley Peterson|chapter=The Ghost|title=Stagestruck|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=w:Key Porter Books|year=2002|page=45|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/stagestruck0000pete/page/45/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-55263-501-8|passage=There were many stories circulating about Dancer {{quote-gloss|a horse}}, some fact, some fiction. Gossip about money offered for him and turned down. Rumors about his '''tempestuous''' disposition. Stories about people who'd tried to ride him and got hurt. Abby hoped that she wouldn't be another of those. If the fall didn't crush her, the disappointment would.}}

